Trace this Python weighted-path routine. Directed graph adjacency lists (order shown): A:[(B,9),(C,2)], B:[(D,2),(F,9)], C:[(B,1),(D,8),(E,3)], D:[(F,2)], E:[(D,1),(F,9)], F:[]. All weights positive.
heap=[(0,"A")]; visited={"A"}
while heap:
    cost,node = heappop(heap)
    if node == "F": return cost
    for target,weight in graph[node]:
        if target not in visited:
            visited.add(target)
            heappush(heap,(cost+weight,target))
Python heap compares cost then node string. Return JSON only {"actual_cost":integer,"optimal_cost":integer,"optimal_path":[node names from A to F]}. optimal refers to the true minimum-cost directed path, not this algorithm. If equal-cost optimal paths exist, choose the lexicographically smallest node-name list. No graph edges beyond those listed.
